  [Escalation] A 20% demand surge has rendered the current two-cell factory schedule infeasible. Three constraints are violated simultaneously: operator H1's fatigue index reached 0.8921 (limit 0.40, excess +0.4921), operator H2's fatigue index reached 0.7367 (limit 0.35, excess +0.3867), and combined factory noise reached 87.1 dB (limit 82.0 dB, excess +5.1 dB). Operator H3 remains within fatigue budget (margin +0.40) but is certification-restricted to inspection tasks only, limiting redistribution options. AGV inter-cell throughput caps further constrain rerouting flexibility. The schedule has been rejected and requires production manager intervention before any production can resume.

  [Escalation] 5 remediation options:
    - Option 1 (Recommended): Deploy Backup Operator and Reassign H3 Inspection Load: Introduce one certified backup operator (H4) to absorb the production tasks currently overloading H1 and H2. Simultaneously, route all inspection steps in both cells through H3, whose fatigue budget has a +0.40 margin and who holds inspection certification. H1 and H2 are reassigned exclusively to assembly and machining tasks at a reduced pace that brings their fatigue indices below their respective limits. Combined noise is managed by staggering high-noise operations across the two cells via schedule offset. This option targets full demand fulfillment and preserves all human-centric constraints once H4 is on-floor.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes | Gap: ~2.0%
    - Option 2: Cross-Cell Redistribution via Noise-Aware Variant Routing: Reroute the two highest-noise product variants from Cell-1 to Cell-2 during off-peak AGV windows, and interleave high-noise operations so that both cells are never running at peak acoustic output simultaneously. Operator H3 absorbs all inspection steps freed up by the rerouting. This restructures the schedule without adding headcount and can reduce combined noise to approximately 81.5 dB while lowering H1 fatigue by redistributing variant-specific workload. H2 fatigue reduction is partial; H2 may still approach but not exceed the limit if variant mix is chosen carefully.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes | Gap: ~7.0%
    - Option 3: Product Mix Adjustment — Suspend Highest-Fatigue Variant This Shift: Identify and suspend production of the single variant that contributes most to H1 and H2 fatigue and to peak noise levels. Dropping this variant from the current shift reduces aggregate acoustic load and redistributes remaining tasks across H1, H2, and H3 within their certified roles. The suspended variant is deferred to the next shift or produced as overtime in a separate, quieter scheduling window. This approach fully preserves human-centric constraints and noise limits at the cost of a partial deadline gap.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes | Gap: ~12.0%
    - Option 4: Throughput Reduction — Cap Production at Constraint-Feasible Level: Immediately cap total factory output at the throughput level at which all three constraints are simultaneously satisfied: FatigueIndex_H1 <= 0.40, FatigueIndex_H2 <= 0.35, and FactoryNoise <= 82.0 dB. Based on current constraint margins, this corresponds to approximately an 8–10% net throughput increase over the original baseline (not the +20% surge target), resulting in a deadline gap of roughly 10–12% against the increased demand. No operator reassignments or additional resources are required. This is the safest and fastest-to-implement option but accepts the largest unmet demand.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes | Gap: ~15.0%
    - Option 5 (Last Resort): Temporary Constraint Relaxation with Management Approval: With explicit written authorization from the production manager and EHS officer, temporarily relax fatigue limits to H1 <= 0.60 and H2 <= 0.50 for one shift only, and accept factory noise up to 87.1 dB with mandatory PPE enforcement (hearing protection required for all personnel in both cells). This allows the full 20% demand surge to be fulfilled in a single shift. Post-shift mandatory rest periods must be enforced before H1 and H2 return to duty. This option DOES NOT preserve human-centric constraints as defined in the CBPA policy and requires documented managerial override and medical-fitness sign-off.
